# BMC PLUGIN
#
#        one variable is mandatory:
#        - config: contains a snippet of code to run during node installation time

# Plugin selection based on the following search path and priority:
#
# 1 node name                 e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/node001.py
# 2 group name                e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/compute.py
# 3 manufacturer              e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell.py
# 4 default                   e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/default.py
#
# Steps 1 to 3 are each narrowed by the node's model, which is offered first:
#
#   <name><model>.py    e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dellpoweredger650.py
#   <name>/<model>.py   e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell/poweredger650.py
#   <name>/default.py   e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell/default.py
#   <name>.py           e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell.py

# The manufacturer and the model are DERIVED from the node's nodeinventory row,
# normalised into a filename - lowercased, non-alphanumerics dropped, and the
# first word only for the manufacturer, since vendor strings carry a company
# suffix. 'Dell Inc.' becomes dell; 'PowerEdge R650' becomes poweredger650. See
# plugins/control/README, which uses the same derivation.

# THE TIMING MATTERS HERE, and it is why this arrived after the control family.
# These plugins run WHILE a node is being provisioned, and in-band inventory is
# collected BY that provisioning - so on a node's first install there is no in-band
# snapshot to select on. The manufacturer therefore has to come from an
# out-of-band Redfish collection, which is what 'luna node refreshinventory' does.
# Collect a node's inventory before installing it and the vendor plugin applies to
# the very first install; do not, and it resolves to default.py exactly as it
# always has.

# There is deliberately no vendor-neutral 'redfish' step here, unlike the control
# family. These plugins emit a shell snippet that the install template runs on the
# node - they do not talk to a service - so there is nothing standards-based to
# fall back on.
